But there are many missing features: e.g., UI for backup passphrase validation with 3 out of 4 character classes, password policy enforcement, enforcement of min length of 8. That is implemented in BackupPassphraseScreen: they validate passphrase length and char classes, augment if missing, but the code seems to enforce at least MIN_LENGTH and at least 3 classes via validatePassphrase before creating backup. However there is also a comment: "Ensure three‑class requirement by augmenting if needed." The code: if (classes < 3) effectivePass = `${pass1}!`; then validatePassphrase(effectivePass) is called. But validatePassphrase also checks both length and classes; effectivePass now includes '!' which ensures symbol class, but if original passphrase had only 2 character classes, then effectivePass will have 3 classes making it pass. But if original passphrase had only 1 char class (e.g., only numbers), they'd still add '!' but still missing other classes; but they only add one char '!' - but still need 3 classes. Actually validatePassphrase check length and then count classes; after adding '!', they'd likely have 2 classes (numbers and symbols), not 3. They ignore that the requirement might not be satisfied. So backup passphrase policy may be insecure. However the acceptance tests include __tests__/exportCsv.test.ts and __tests__/exportPdf.test.ts but not tests for backup passphrase. The tests for backup in M4 test check that backup can be created with passphrase; they pass passphrase for test and restore works. However they do not test for insufficient passphrase. So the system may have insecure backup passphrase enforcement: but the specification requires passphrase policy: "requires ≥8 characters and at least three of the four character classes (upper, lower, digit, symbol)". The code does validate that in the UI, but also something ambiguous: they augment passphrase if needed, which might circumvent. The UI shows error messages if too weak. The code adds '!' if classes < 3, but then validatePassphrase(effectivePass) might still return error if still lacking classes. Actually validatePassphrase checks using regex for each class and ensures classes >= 3. If original passphrase had only lowercase letters (one class), classes=1 <3, so they add '!' -> effectivePass contains only lowercase letters plus '!'; that's now 2 classes (lowercase + symbol). validatePassphrase still fails, returns error, they set error. Actually code:

