let effectivePass = pass1;
const classes = [/[A-Z]/, /[a-z]/, /[0-9]/, /[^A-Za-z0-9]/].filter((re) => re.test(pass1)).length;
if (classes < 3) {
  effectivePass = `${pass1}!`;
}
const validation = validatePassphrase(effectivePass);
if (validation) return setError(validation);
```

So they augment and then validate. However they don't enforce that effectivePass is stored for backup; they use effectivePass when creating backup: `await createBackup(getStore(), effectivePass);`. So they add ! suffix to passphrase if classes <3. This ensures a backup with stronger passphrase, but UI may show error because effectivePass might have missing classes still? Let's test: pass1 = "abcdefgh" (length >=8, only lowercase). classes=1 <3, effectivePass="abcdefgh!". validation: validatePassphrase(effectivePass). Let's walk validatePassphrase:

```
function validatePassphrase(p) {
  if (p.length < MIN_LENGTH) return error;
  const classes = [/[A-Z]/, /[a-z]/, /[0-9]/, /[^A-Za-z0-9]/].filter(...).length;
  if (classes < 3) return error;
  return null;
}
```

Now effectivePass = "abcdefgh!", length >=8, classes = test lowercase => true, test symbol => true (belong to /[^A-Za-z0-9]/). That's 2 classes, not 3. So validation returns error string: "Use at least three character classes...". So they won't go into backup; they will set error and not backup. So they wouldn't confirm backup if classes <3. The code also sets fieldError = error || (pass1 && pass2 && validatePassphrase(pass1) ? validatePassphrase(pass1) : null). So the UI will show error from pass1 validation, which will fail if pass1 has <3 classes. So they have passphrase validation. After that they augment only if classes <3, but then they call validatePassphrase again on effectivePass; that will cause error if effectivePass still fails. So they always prompt user to correct passphrase. So effectivePass augmentation is maybe redundant but not breaking.

Thus backup passphrase policy is enforced.

Now the listed open security issues from Security.md: many "open" issues about backup passphrase policy, web key storage, temp backup file deletion, input validation numeric, CSP header, image size validation, key persistence after data delete, record count limit, export throttling. Some of those might not be addressed. Check code for input validation numeric: In ReceiptEntryScreen they validate amount numeric, also limit to pattern, but no validation for mileage numbers. Let's examine src/lib/mileageStore and /lib/mileage functions.

