message.reasoning_content:
The task is to review the solution of a codebase. The acceptance tests passed, and static validation passed. However, the Security report found many warnings (open issues). The reviewer should only require functional correctness? The instruction says: "Judge whether the solution fully and correctly solves the task, including edge cases the acceptance tests may miss. Only ask for a revision for real defects, not style. You may approve only when the acceptance tests passed and static validation passed; if either failed, the verdict is revise and your issues must say what the coder has to fix to make them pass."

Thus, even though security vulnerabilities exist, they are warnings, not blocking. They are not "high/critical" at the level causing static validation to fail. The static validation includes trivy dependency vulnerabilities (other) which gave warnings. Since it's not failing (ok: true), and acceptance tests passed, we could approve. However, we must consider if any functional defects that tests didn't catch (like backup file cleanup or passphrase policy). The acceptance tests include tests for backup passphrase policy (but only checks invalid passphrase case). They do not test that backup passphrase must meet certain criteria. In the code, validation is done in BackupPassphraseScreen, not pure function level. However security checker flagged that passphrase policy is possibly insufficient, but they added logic to ensure at least MIN_LENGTH and three classes. The code ensures that if classes <3, effectivePass is appended with "!" to meet classes >=? It adds "!" which covers at least one more class (symbol). This ensures three classes, maybe meeting requirement. So functional has validation.

We also have other warnings: backup temporary file deletion is done in saveAndShare (clean up after sharing). However the backup.ts does not delete temporary file after restore is done (no removal). In backup.ts, after creating backup, they return base64 string and don't write a temporary file; the code in BackupPassphraseScreen uses saveAndShare to write backup file, which deletes after sharing. That's fine.

